@h1 The annotated essay h1@

Read one complete, verified document end to end and understand every construct in
it. This is the payoff page that ties the thesis, table, and creative-writing
recipes together — a real 53-line essay that builds to a three-page PDF, walked
block by block.

@aside(class note) 💡 The whole file is core vocabulary. Nothing here needs a
definition, because every construct — headings, emphasis, a table, a figure, a
list — is a built-in node. This is the smallest realistic document that exercises
a writer's whole toolkit. aside@

@h2 The title h2@

@@@pre
@h1 Let Them Sleep: The Case for Later School Start Times h1@
pre@@@

The opening @strong h1 strong@ becomes the document title. The colon and the rest
of the line are ordinary text inside it.

@h2 The byline h2@

Three source lines, each ending in the void break node:

@@@pre
Jordan Ellis @br br@
Ms. Rivera — English 11 @br br@
5 July 2026
pre@@@

These collapse into @strong one strong@ paragraph joined by line breaks.
Consecutive non-blank lines are a single paragraph; the break node is how you
force a visible line break inside one. It is a void element — it has no body.

@h2 The epigraph h2@

@@@pre
@blockquote "Sleep is the golden chain that ties health…" — Thomas Dekker blockquote@
pre@@@

A @strong blockquote strong@ sets the quotation apart. The straight quotes and the
em dash pass through exactly as typed.

@h2 The thesis statement h2@

A plain paragraph — no wrapping node, no indentation. This is the core Wit move:
prose is prose. It closes with the argument's three-part roadmap in ordinary
sentences.

@h2 A section heading h2@

@@@pre
@h2 The Teenage Clock Runs Late h2@
pre@@@

Every section uses the same shape. Body paragraphs follow, and one of them ends
with an inline emphasis:

@@@pre
… the rest their developing brain requires @em every single night em@.
pre@@@

The @strong em strong@ node and the @code _…_ code@ mark are interchangeable;
this file uses the node form here and a @strong strong strong@ node a few lines
later.

@h2 The data table h2@

@@@pre
@table |caption Recommended versus actual sleep for adolescents| |rows [[Group, Recommended sleep, Typical school night], [Teens 13 to 18, 8 to 10 hours, 6.5 to 7 hours]]|
pre@@@

The @strong first strong@ inner array is the header row and becomes @code th code@
cells; each later array is a table row of @code td code@. Caption and rows ride in
parameters; cells are comma-separated @em within a row array em@ — which is safe.
The comma restriction applies only to record fields inside braces, never to table
rows.

@h2 The captioned figure h2@

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src assets/attendance.svg| |alt Bar chart showing attendance rising from 82 to 94 percent| |size medium|
  @figcaption Figure 1. On-time attendance before and after a later start. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

@strong figure strong@ centres its contents by default. @code size medium code@
maps to a 380-pixel maximum width (the presets are @code small code@,
@code medium code@, @code large code@, and @code full code@, or a number or
percentage). The @code alt code@ text is required, and the @code src code@ path is
relative to the essay file.

@h2 The conclusion h2@

Another @strong h2 strong@ and a single closing paragraph. No new construct — a
reminder that most of a document is just prose under headings.

@h2 The Works Cited list h2@

@@@pre
@ol
  @li American Academy of Pediatrics. "School Start Times…" @em Pediatrics em@, vol. 134, no. 3, 2014. li@
  @li Dekker, Thomas. @em The Pleasant Comedy of Patient Grissell em@. 1603. li@
ol@
pre@@@

Each @strong li strong@ is one entry; @strong em strong@ italicises the source
title inside it. Straight quotes render as entities and the en dash passes
through.

@h2 Build and open h2@

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit -o essay.pdf
pre@@@

The finished file builds to a three-page, 158,820-byte PDF (verified). For HTML
instead, use @code -o essay.html code@; to inspect the raw markup, add
@code --fragment code@.

@h2 What the whole file teaches h2@

Flat prose is the default. Headings, emphasis, a table, a figure, and a list are
each @strong one node strong@. Nothing needs a definition because every construct
is core vocabulary — and that is the whole point.

